# Example 13: DNA data storage
# Encodes digital information into DNA sequences and (conceptually) reads it back
#
# Workflow:
#   1. Binary data -> base encoding (e.g., Erlich's Goldman coding, with rotation + redundancy)
#   2. Synthesize oligos -> physical DNA
#   3. Sequencing -> alignment & decoding -> restore original bytes
#
# This .helix file provides a DNA carrier context (any #gene block acts as a "storage sequence");
# the actual encoding/decoding runs via the Python API:

#promoter name=p_storage strength=-0.2

# Carrier sequence (illustrative, not real encoded data)
#gene name=storage_payload promoter=p_storage
ATG GCT GGT GCT GGT GCT GGT TAA
#end

#regulate p_storage -> storage_payload strength=+0.5

